"The Ace of Spades" <julianwaldby@comcast.net> writes:

> I think Andrew should have been stronger with his words. In pseudo-random
> number generation, each integer is guaranteed to come up once in every
> MAX_RAND turns, where MAX_RAND is the size of the pseudo-random number
> generation pool. 

What's the "generation pool"?  Consider the simplest 
out-of-the-hat generator, addition of two former 
numbers modulo 10: 

Seed is the numbers 2, 3. 

The sequence built is 

2 3 5 8 3 1 4 5 9 4 3 7 0 7 0 7 0 

and so on.  [This is an incredibly stupid and bad RNG, 
of course.]  Could you please explain what the 'generation 
pool' is, if not {2,3}, and why in this case MAX_RAND is 
obviously  = oo?  

> Your comment about "unlikely" is not appropriate here,
> because the result could be determined, since pseudo-random sequences are
> deterministic. 

Whatever 'deterministic' may mean here.  If I perform 
10^{10} spin measures, write the results down and use 
this as my RNG sequence forever, the sequence is somehow 
'deterministic', although created via an experiment 
which physicists mostly consider to have 'truly randomly 
generated' results.  IMHO, the notion of 'deterministic' 
or 'random' is only meaningful when talking about a process, 
not its results.  

> I believe someone said that the number cycle has a period of
> 10^10, or in that region. This means that there will be no probabilities
> below 0.0000000001, for starters, 

I don't really understand what you mean by stating 
that 'there are no probabilities', since probabilities 
of events are just numbers assigned to events, and, to a 
given measure, any measurable event has a probability.  
But if you mean that the RNG sequence cannot satisfy an 
event which has, under the assumption of a true random 
generation, a probability of < 10^{-10}, this is of course 
wrong.  Consider the event "The sequence from start to 
$RNG-Cyclelength matches exactly $RNG-Outcome".  This event 
clearly has probability 10^{-10^{10}}, and takes of course 
place for the RNG-generated sequence.  [Replace $RNG-Cyclelength 
by any number N to get an event of probability 10^{-N} which 
the RNG outcome matches.]  

> Therefore, there is no element of chance in this problem, just the
> appearance of chance, and the decision on whether or not this is a viable
> means of indefinite survival could be determined as true or false, given
